dwc2: drop a stale USBSUSP flag instead of signalling a suspend

Applied by `make` on top of the pinned upstream commit, see the Makefile.

The driver masks USBSUSPM from a suspend until the next WKUINT. A further
suspend entry in that window sets GINTSTS.USBSUSP without an interrupt, and
the unmask on wakeup then delivers it right after RESUME, on a bus that is
running again: the stack ends up suspended for good, and every later wakeup
repeats the pattern. Seen on an AT32F405 behind a hub across a host sleep.
DSTS.SUSPSTS is the live state, so a flag seen while the core is not in
suspend is stale and only cleared.

diff --git a/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c b/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c
index f13372532..ffe82ae31 100644
--- a/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c
+++ b/src/portable/synopsys/dwc2/dcd_dwc2.c
@@ -1256,8 +1256,18 @@ void dcd_int_handler(uint8_t rhport) {
 
   if (gintsts & GINTSTS_USBSUSP) {
     dwc2->gintsts = GINTSTS_USBSUSP;
-    dwc2->gintmsk &= ~GINTMSK_USBSUSPM;
-    dcd_event_bus_signal(rhport, DCD_EVENT_SUSPEND, true);
+    // USBSUSP records the entry into suspend, it is not a level. It is masked
+    // from here until the next WKUINT, and a further suspend entry during that
+    // time (seen on an AT32F405 behind a hub across a host sleep) sets the
+    // flag again without an interrupt. The unmask on wakeup then delivers it
+    // right after RESUME, on a bus that is running again, and the stack stays
+    // suspended for good: every later wakeup repeats the pattern. DSTS.SUSPSTS
+    // is the live state, so a flag seen while the core is not in suspend is
+    // stale and only cleared.
+    if (dwc2->dsts & DSTS_SUSPSTS) {
+      dwc2->gintmsk &= ~GINTMSK_USBSUSPM;
+      dcd_event_bus_signal(rhport, DCD_EVENT_SUSPEND, true);
+    }
   }
 
   if (gintsts & GINTSTS_WKUINT) {
